--- abstract

This document defines IKEv2 extensions that enable an egress security gateway to notify its ingress counterpart when IP fragmentation is detected on the tunnel link or when a received packet is too large for IPsec decapsulation.

Two notification types are introduced.
The Link Maximum Atomic Packet (LMAP) notification conveys the IP version and fragment length observed at the egress node, enabling the ingress node to derive the largest packet size that traverses the tunnel link without fragmentation.
The Packet Too Big (PTB) notification conveys the Link MTU (LMTU) and the effective MTU to receive (EMTU_R) of the egress node, enabling the ingress node to determine the maximum packet size that the egress node can accept for IPsec processing.

Together, these notifications enable the ingress node to set its Tunnel MTU (TMTU) so that reassembly at the egress node is avoided and oversized packets are not transmitted.

--- middle

# Introduction {#sec1}

## Problem Statement {#sec-problem}

Reassembling IP fragments at the egress security gateway of an IPsec tunnel is resource-intensive and fragile.
As detailed in {{?RFC4963}}, {{!RFC6864}}, and {{!RFC8900}}, IP fragmentation does not scale well at high data rates: the 16-bit IPv4 identification field is too small to prevent fragment misassociation, and hardware-accelerated security gateways may be unable to process reassembled packets that exceed the capacity of their crypto engine.

Despite these problems, existing mechanisms are insufficient to prevent fragmentation:

* Setting the IPv4 Don't Fragment bit (DF=1) avoids fragmentation but introduces black-hole risks.
  On-path routers that cannot forward the packet silently drop it if they do not generate ICMP Packet Too Big (PTB) messages, or if such messages are blocked by firewalls (see {{sec-df1}} for a detailed discussion).

* ICMP PTB messages, even when delivered, may not carry enough information for the ingress node to identify the affected Security Association (SA).
  When ESP is encapsulated in UDP or TCP, the "Internet Header + 64 bits of Original Data" field in the ICMPv4 PTB message contains the UDP or TCP header rather than the ESP SPI (see {{sec-df1}}).

* The egress node's effective MTU to receive (EMTU_R) — the maximum size of an IPsec-protected packet that the egress node can accept for decapsulation — is a local configuration parameter that is not communicated by any existing mechanism.

## Approach

This document addresses these gaps by defining two IKEv2 notification types that are exchanged over the authenticated IKEv2 channel:

* A Link Maximum Atomic Packet (LMAP) notification, sent when the egress node detects that a received Tunnel Link Packet (TLP) has been fragmented.
  This notification conveys the IP version and fragment length, enabling the ingress node to derive the Tunnel Maximum Atomic Packet (TMAP) — the largest Tunnel Transit Packet (TTP) size that avoids fragmentation on the tunnel link.

* A Packet Too Big (PTB) notification, sent when a received (and potentially reassembled) TLP exceeds the EMTU_R and cannot be decapsulated.
  This notification conveys both the LMTU and EMTU_R, enabling the ingress node to set its TMTU so that future packets are neither fragmented nor dropped.

The LMAP and PTB notifications are carried exclusively over the authenticated IKEv2 channel.
Any ICMP PTB messages that the egress node may independently generate are a separate mechanism and are not relied upon by this specification.

Upon receiving these notifications, the ingress node adjusts its TMTU and, for IPv4 TTP with DF=0, performs inner fragmentation to prevent reassembly at the egress node.

## Scope

This document is focused on providing fragmentation-related information from the egress node to the ingress node and specifying how the ingress node uses this information.
This document does not define a Path MTU Discovery (PMTUD) procedure {{!RFC1191}} or a Packetization Layer PMTUD (PLPMTUD) procedure {{!RFC4821}}.
PLPMTUD for IPsec has been explored in {{?I-D.spiriyath-ipsecme-dynamic-ipsec-pmtu}}.
End-to-end PMTUD between the original source and destination is independent and out of scope.

The extension follows {{!RFC8900}}'s recommendation that each layer should handle fragmentation at its own layer and reduce reliance on IP fragmentation to the greatest degree possible.

## Terminology Overview

{{fig-frag}} depicts various fragmentation scenarios that can occur when Tunnel Transit Packets (TTP) are encapsulated over an IPsec tunnel.
The IPsec packets exchanged between the ingress and the egress security gateway are designated as Tunnel Link Packets (TLP).

~~~
Source          Security             Security             Destination
or              Gateway              Gateway              or
Sender       (Ingress node)        (Egress node)          Receiver

+--+             +---+                 +---+              +---+
|  |  +  +  +    |   |  +  +  +  +  +  |   |  +  +  +  +  |   |
+--+  routers    +---+    routers      +---+  routers     +---+
                  <--------------------->
                            N
+---+---+----+                            +---+---+----+
|IPs|IPd|Data|  Tunnel Transit Packet     |IPs|IPd|Data|
+---+---+----+         (TTP)              +---+---+----+



1) No fragmentation

                +---+---+---+---+---+----+
                |IPi|IPe|ESP|IPs|IPd|Data| Tunnel Link Packet
                +---+---+---+---+---+----+       (TLP)

2) Mid-tunnel fragmentation (performed by a router on N)
  (only for IPv4 DF=0 TLP)
                         +---+---+---+---+---+--+
                         |IPi|IPe|ESP|IPs|IPd|Da| (TLP)
                         +---+---+---+---+---+--+
                     +---+---+--+
                     |IPi|IPe|ta| (TLP)
                     +---+---+--+

3) Inner fragmentation (performed by the Ingress node)
  (only for IPv4 DF=0 TTP)

                +---+---+---+---+---+--+
                |IPi|IPe|ESP|IPs|IPd|Da| (TLP)
                +---+---+---+---+---+--+
            +---+---+---+---+---+--+
            |IPi|IPe|ESP|IPs|IPd|ta| (TLP)
            +---+---+---+---+---+--+

