   (IPv4 or IPv6 TLP)
                +---+---+---+---+---+--+
                |IPi|IPe|ESP|IPs|IPd|Da| (TLP)
                +---+---+---+---+---+--+
            +---+---+--+
            |IPi|IPe|ta| (TLP)
            +---+---+--+

5) Source fragmentation
  (IPv6 or IPv4)
    +---+---+--+
    |IPs|IPd|Da|  (TTP)
    +---+---+--+
+---+---+--+
|IPs|IPd|ta|
+---+---+--+

~~~
{: #fig-frag title="Illustration of Different Types of Fragmentation.
IPs (resp. IPi, IPe, IPd) designates the IP address of the Source (resp. the Ingress node, the Egress node, the Destination).
The IPsec tunnel is considered as an ESP tunnel.
The IP payload is represented as 'Data'.
Fragmentation is illustrated by splicing 'Data' into 'Da' and 'ta'.
The figure does not distinguish encrypted from unencrypted Data; the presence of the ESP header indicates the payload is encrypted." }

Reassembly is performed by the egress node in two cases.
First, when mid-tunnel fragmentation occurs (see (2) in {{fig-frag}}) — in which case the TLP outer header uses IPv4 with its Don't Fragment bit set to 0 (DF=0).
Second, when outer fragmentation is performed by the ingress node (see (4) in {{fig-frag}}).
The main difference is that with outer fragmentation, the ingress node is aware that the egress will perform reassembly.
In both cases, reassembly of the TLP does not prevent the TTP from being processed by IPsec, unless the reassembled TLP exceeds the EMTU_R.

{{fig-esp-frag}} summarizes the operations performed by the egress node as a function of the TLP size.
The optimal TTP size is the maximum that avoids fragmentation; the corresponding TTP size is designated as the Tunnel Maximum Atomic Packet (TMAP).
The resulting TLP (after IPsec encapsulation) is the Link Maximum Atomic Packet (LMAP).
The difference between TMAP and LMAP is the IPsec encapsulation overhead.

* A TLP smaller than the LMAP is forwarded without fragmentation.
* A TLP larger than the LMAP but smaller than the EMTU_R is fragmented and reassembled; the TTP is transmitted.
* A TLP (potentially reassembled) larger than the EMTU_R cannot be processed by IPsec and is dropped.

~~~
          TMAP              TMTU
+---------+-------------------+--------------> TTP
|         |encap              |encap
|         |<--->|             |<--->|
| No frag. (1)  |  Fragmentation    |
|               |  (2) Mid-tunnel   |  Packet Too Big
|               |  (4) Outer        |
+---------------+-------------------+--------> IPsec encapsulated TTP
              LMAP                EMTU_R
~~~
{: #fig-esp-frag title="Fragmentation and Packet Too Big as a function of TTP/TLP size. 'encap' designates the IPsec overhead." }

This document enables the egress node to inform the ingress node that:

* a received TLP has been fragmented (via an LMAP notification), and
* a received TLP is too large to be processed by IPsec (via a PTB notification).

As depicted in {{fig-overview}}, by supporting this extension the ingress and egress nodes cooperate to minimize reassembly at the egress node.
The ingress node limits the use of outer fragmentation and sets its TMTU so that TTP are not subject to fragmentation or reassembly.
For TTP with IPv4 addresses and DF=0, the ingress node performs inner fragmentation (3) to prevent reassembly at the egress node.

The mechanism is especially useful when the tunnel outer IP addresses are IPv4 with DF=0, as mid-tunnel fragmentation may occur without the ingress node being aware of it.

The PTB notification also addresses the case where a TLP exceeds the EMTU_R.
A TLP may exceed the EMTU_R even when individual fragments are within the LMTU: with IPv4 DF=0, the egress node may receive fragments that individually fit within the LMTU (and thus do not trigger ICMP PTB) but reassemble into a packet that exceeds the EMTU_R.
Similarly, the LMTU of the network may exceed the processing capacity of the IPsec hardware.

Although the LMTU values may also be conveyed via ICMP PTB messages, communicating them via IKEv2 notifications may provide some additional advantages.
The IKE notification MAY be sent together with the TTP ICMP PTB sent by the router.
This may improve the network latency and optimize the use of the security gateway resources, especially when the SA has been set for multiple Sources.
The TTP ICMP PTB sent by the egress router is sent specifically to the sending source, and so one can expect the ICMP PTB being sent to every source.
By sending the notification via IKE, the information is received by the ingress Security Gateway which can take action globally for all Sources associated to the SA.
This prevents large packets greater than the MTU from being encrypted and sent to the egress router unnecessarily.
However, these advantages are only secondary and are only provided by the egress interface; the mechanism cannot be used by any other node and so cannot be used for PMTU discovery.
This document does not discuss these optimizations further.

